<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/harthacnut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Harthacnut</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Harthacnut was king of Denmark from 1035 and of England from 1040 to 1042, the son of Cnut the Great and Emma of Normandy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Harun al-Rashid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Harun al-Rashid was Abbasid caliph who reigned from 786 to 809.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/harvard-university.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Harvard University</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Harvard University, founded as Harvard College in 1636 by vote of the General Court of the Massachusetts Bay Colony, is the oldest institution of higher learning in the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/harvester-case"><span class="enc-name">Harvester case</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Harvester case of 1907 was a decision of Justice H.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/haryana"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/haryana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Haryana</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Haryana is a state of northern India surrounding Delhi on three sides, with Chandigarh, shared with Punjab, as its capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/haryanka-dynasty"><span class="enc-name">Haryanka dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Haryanka dynasty ruled the Kingdom of Magadha in the Ganges valley in the 6th and 5th centuries BCE, according to Buddhist and Jain sources.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/harz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/harz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Harz</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Harz is the highest mountain range of northern Germany, spread across Lower Saxony, Saxony-Anhalt and Thuringia, with the Brocken (1,141 metres) as its highest peak.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/harzburg"><span class="enc-name">Harzburg</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Harzburg was a royal castle built by Emperor Henry IV from about 1065 on a hill above present-day Bad Harzburg in the Harz mountains.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/harzgerode"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/harzgerode.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Harzgerode</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Harzgerode is a small town in the eastern Harz mountains of Saxony-Anhalt, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-agha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-agha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasan Agha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan Agha was a Sardinian-born convert and eunuch who rose in the service of Hayreddin Barbarossa and governed Algiers as his deputy from 1533.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-kalbi"><span class="enc-name">Hasan al-Kalbi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan al-Kalbi was the first emir of Sicily of the Kalbid dynasty, appointed by the Fatimid caliph al-Mansur in 948 to restore order on the island.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-utrush"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-al-utrush.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasan al-Utrush</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan al-Utrush, called al-Nasir al-Kabir, was a Zaydi imam and Alid ruler in Tabaristan on the Caspian coast of Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-bughra-khan"><span class="enc-name">Hasan Bughra Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan Bughra Khan, also called Harun, was a ruler of the Kara-Khanid Khanate in Central Asia in the late 10th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasan Buzurg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan Buzurg ('Hasan the Tall') was a Mongol emir of the Jalayir tribe and founder of the Jalayirid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali"><span class="enc-name">Hasan ibn Ali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan ibn Ali was a grandson of the Prophet Muhammad and the elder son of Ali, the fourth Rashidun caliph.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-hasan-ibn-kannun"><span class="enc-name">al-Hasan ibn Kannun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Hasan ibn Kannun was the last Idrisid ruler in Morocco, holding territory in the north of the country in the mid-10th century between the Fatimids and the Umayyad…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasan ibn Zayd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan ibn Zayd was a descendant of the Prophet's grandson Hasan, invited in 864 by the people of Ruyan and Tabaristan, angered by Tahirid land seizures, to lead them.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ii-of-alamut"><span class="enc-name">Hasan II of Alamut</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan II was the fourth ruler of the Nizari Ismaili state of Alamut in northern Iran, from 1162 to 1166.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-juri"><span class="enc-name">Hasan Juri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan Juri was a Shia Sufi preacher in Khorasan, a disciple of Shaykh Khalifa, whose followers spread a message of resistance to unjust rule and expectation of the Mahdi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-kuchak"><span class="enc-name">Hasan Kuchak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan Kuchak, 'Little Hasan', was a grandson of the Ilkhanid commander Chupan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-pasha-of-baghdad"><span class="enc-name">Hasan Pasha (governor of Baghdad)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan Pasha was an Ottoman governor of Baghdad from 1704 until his death in 1723.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-pasha-son-of-hayreddin"><span class="enc-name">Hasan Pasha (son of Hayreddin)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan Pasha was a son of Hayreddin Barbarossa who served three terms as Ottoman beylerbey of Algiers between 1544 and 1567.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-i-sabbah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-i-sabbah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasan-i Sabbah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan-i Sabbah was an Ismaili missionary who founded the Nizari branch after the succession crisis of 1094 in the Fatimid Caliphate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasan-jalal-dawla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-jalal-dawla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasan-Jalal Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasan-Jalal Dawla was the Armenian prince of Khachen, in present-day Karabakh, from 1214 until his death in 1261.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasanak"><span class="enc-name">Hasanak</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasanak was the vizier of the Ghaznavid sultan Mahmud of Ghazni from about 1024 and a supporter of Mahmud's son Muhammad in the succession dispute of 1030.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasankeyf"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasankeyf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasankeyf</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasankeyf, medieval Hisn Kayfa, is an ancient town on the Tigris in south-eastern Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasanlu-lovers"><span class="enc-name">Hasanlu Lovers</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hasanlu Lovers are two human skeletons found in 1972 at Hasanlu Tepe in north-western Iran, lying together in an apparent embrace in a bin where they died when the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayh-ibn-husayn"><span class="enc-name">Hasanwayh ibn Husayn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasanwayh ibn Husayn was a chief of the Kurdish Barzikani tribe who founded the Hasanwayhid dynasty in the western Zagros.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasanwayhids"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasanwayhids.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasanwayhids</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hasanwayhids were a Kurdish dynasty that ruled parts of the central Zagros, including Dinawar and Kermanshah, from about 959 to 1015.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasdai-ibn-shaprut"><span class="enc-name">Hasdai ibn Shaprut</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasdai ibn Shaprut was a Jewish physician, diplomat and patron at the court of the caliphs Abd al-Rahman III and al-Hakam II in Córdoba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasdingi"><span class="enc-name">Hasdingi</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hasdingi were one of the two main branches of the Vandals, a Germanic people.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-barca"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-barca.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasdrubal Barca</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasdrubal Barca was a Carthaginian general, son of Hamilcar Barca and brother of Hannibal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco"><span class="enc-name">Hasdrubal Gisco</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasdrubal Gisco was a Carthaginian general of the Second Punic War who fought the Romans in Iberia until Scipio defeated him at Ilipa in 206 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasdrubal the Fair</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasdrubal the Fair was a Carthaginian general, son-in-law of Hamilcar Barca, who succeeded him as commander in Iberia in about 229 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasekura-tsunenaga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasekura-tsunenaga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasekura Tsunenaga</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasekura Tsunenaga was a samurai in the service of Date Masamune who led a Japanese embassy to Spain and Rome from 1613 to 1620.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hashid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hashid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hashid</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hashid are one of the two great tribal confederations of northern Yemen, alongside the Bakil, living in the highlands north of Sanaa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hashim-al-atassi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hashim-al-atassi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hashim al-Atassi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hashim al-Atassi was a Syrian nationalist politician from Homs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hashim-ibn-suraqa"><span class="enc-name">Hashim ibn Suraqa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hashim ibn Suraqa was an Arab governor of Derbent on the Caspian coast of the Caucasus who in 869, as Abbasid authority weakened, founded the hereditary Hashimid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hashimids-of-derbent"><span class="enc-name">Hashimids of Derbent</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hashimids of Derbent were an Arab dynasty that ruled the city of Derbent on the Caspian coast as hereditary emirs from 869 to 1075.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hashimoto-sanai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hashimoto-sanai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hashimoto Sanai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hashimoto Sanai was a Japanese physician and reformist samurai of the Fukui domain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasidism"><span class="enc-name">Hasidism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Hasidism is a Jewish religious movement founded in the eighteenth century in Eastern Europe, emphasising mysticism, joyful worship and devotion to charismatic leaders.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hasmonean dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hasmonean dynasty ruled Judea in the second and first centuries BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ali-bayoomi"><span class="enc-name">Hassan Ali Bayoomi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hassan Ali Bayoomi was an Aden politician and journalist who served as chief minister of the colony and then of the State of Aden in 1963.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hassan Gouled Aptidon was an Issa Somali politician who represented French Somaliland in the French parliament and later led the campaign for independence.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ibn-al-numan"><span class="enc-name">Hassan ibn al-Nu'man</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hassan ibn al-Nu'man was an Arab general of the Umayyad Caliphate who completed the conquest of North Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ibn-thabit"><span class="enc-name">Hassan ibn Thabit</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hassan ibn Thabit was an Arab poet of the Khazraj tribe of Medina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassan-ii-of-morocco"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hassan-ii-of-morocco.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hassan II of Morocco</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hassan II was King of Morocco from 1961 until his death in 1999, of the Alaouite dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassan-tower"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hassan-tower.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hassan Tower</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hassan Tower is the unfinished minaret of a great mosque in Rabat, Morocco, begun under the Almohad caliph Yaqub al-Mansur in the late twelfth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassanal-bolkiah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hassanal-bolkiah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hassanal Bolkiah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hassanal Bolkiah is the sultan of Brunei, who succeeded his father Omar Ali Saifuddien III in 1967.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hassaniya-arabic"><span cl