he Saxon count Liudolf and his wife Oda.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hats-party"><span class="enc-name">Hats (party)</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hats were one of the two political parties of Sweden's Age of Liberty, dominated by the nobility and officers and in power from 1738 to 1765.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hatshepsut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hatshepsut</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hatshepsut was a female pharaoh of the Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt who ruled c. 1479–1458 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hatti"><span class="enc-name">Hatti</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hatti was the ancient name for the land of central Anatolia that formed the core of the Hittite kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hattians"><span class="enc-name">Hattians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hattians were the earlier inhabitants of central Anatolia, speaking a non-Indo-European language, before the rise of the Hittites.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hattic-language"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hattic-language.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hattic language</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Hattic was the language of the Hattians of central Anatolia in the 3rd and early 2nd millennium BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hattusa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hattusa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hattusa was the capital of the Hittite Empire, located near present-day Boğazkale in central Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusili-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hattusili I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hattusili I was an early king of the Hittites, regarded as one of the founders of the Hittite kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hattusili III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hattusili III was a king of the Hittite Empire in the thirteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hatuey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hatuey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hatuey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hatuey was a Taíno chief from Hispaniola who fled to eastern Cuba and led resistance against the Spanish conquest under Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hatun-xauxa"><span class="enc-name">Hatun Xauxa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hatun Xauxa was an Inca provincial centre in the Mantaro valley of central Peru, near the modern town of Jauja.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hatuncolla"><span class="enc-name">Hatuncolla</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hatuncolla is a town in the Puno region of southern Peru, north-west of Lake Titicaca and near the city of Puno.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hatuniye-madrasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hatu