e">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hawwara are a Berber tribal confederation, originally of Tripolitania in present-day Libya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/haya-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/haya-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Haya people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Haya are a Bantu-speaking people of the Kagera region in north-western Tanzania, on the western shore of Lake Victoria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hayagriva-madhava-temple"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hayagriva-madhava-temple.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hayagriva Madhava Temple</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hayagriva Madhava Temple is a Hindu temple on the Monikut hill at Hajo in Assam, India, dedicated to Vishnu in his horse-headed form, Hayagriva.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hayam-wuruk"><span class="enc-name">Hayam Wuruk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hayam Wuruk was king of Majapahit who reigned from 1350 to 1389 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hayashi-razan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hayashi Razan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Hayashi Razan was a Japanese Neo-Confucian scholar who served the first four Tokugawa shoguns as adviser and drafter of documents.</span></a></li></ul>
